WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.920
It was completely predictable that Republican&nbsp;
Senator Ted Cruz's approval rating was going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:13.680
to fall among Democrats and among left leaning&nbsp;
independents after he in a completely I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.200
I don't know what he was thinking, but he well,&nbsp;
actually, I do know what he was thinking. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:22.960
fled with his family to Cancun when the power&nbsp;
went out in his home state of Texas. And what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.840
he was thinking was quite clear, which is, I&nbsp;
want to have power. Let's go somewhere where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.720
it's warm and there's power. That's exactly what&nbsp;
he was thinking until it completely blew up in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:38.720
face. And so we were expecting that is already&nbsp;
low approval among Democrats and left leaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:46.240
independents was going to fall. But Ted Cruz's&nbsp;
approval rating has collapsed 20 points among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:53.120
Republican voters. And this is really a&nbsp;
signal of the worst situation for Ted Cruz&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:01:01.680
since he took office in 2013. Quite frankly,&nbsp;
according to a Yahoo! YouGov poll, 24 percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:10.160
of voters approve of him overall and 49 percent&nbsp;
disapprove. That's a net approval of minus 25 for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:16.400
Ted Cruz. But that's overall. And when you look at&nbsp;
the Republican numbers in a morning consult poll,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:25.360
Ted Cruz is at 53 percent approval among&nbsp;
Republicans. That's down 23 points from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:33.120
January on January 9th. After the Trump riots,&nbsp;
76 percent of Republicans supported Ted Cruz.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:40.240
And that number is now down 23 points to 53&nbsp;
percent Republican support for Ted Cruz. Nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:46.320
half of the Republican Party does not&nbsp;
approve of Ted Cruz. And that is absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:54.320
stunning. Ted Cruz's approval was already sort&nbsp;
of on the decline in January. If you go back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:02:02.080
to December, you see that in the period from&nbsp;
December 27 to January five, Cruz's approval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:10.000
was 81 percent among Republicans. Then January&nbsp;
six to nine, the immediate aftermath of the riot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:19.360
he was down from 81 to 76 percent approval among&nbsp;
Republicans and then now 53 percent approval among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:26.480
Republicans. Now, for us, the unfortunate reality,&nbsp;
and it's been a reality that's much bigger than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.760
Ted Cruz for a long time. Ted Cruz isn't up for&nbsp;
reelection until twenty twenty four. He just won&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:37.760
in twenty eighteen six year terms for senators,&nbsp;
up again in twenty twenty four. That's an insanely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:43.200
long amount of time for this to have any effect&nbsp;
electorally. I know that we can hope that voters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:49.200
will remember this in twenty twenty four. I&nbsp;
think it's just too long. The circumstances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:56.000
in twenty, twenty four for Ted Cruz are going&nbsp;
to be far more dictated by what's going on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:03.920
at the time. Is Trump the Republican nominee or&nbsp;
not. Other factors at that point and if this were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:09.920
to have an effect, it would be getting Ted Cruz&nbsp;
to resign. Now, there seems to be no chance that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:15.200
that happens. I've seen no indication whatsoever,&nbsp;
no reason to think Ted Cruz is going to resign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:23.120
over this the way out. Of Ted Cruz as a senator in&nbsp;
Texas is to register new voters and to get out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:29.120
vote, it's not to convert existing Republican&nbsp;
voters. What we've learned many times is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:35.040
if you rely on Republican voters to remember the&nbsp;
horrible things that their elected officials did.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:40.800
You don't win, it's just not a winning&nbsp;
strategy, is what we've learned over time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:47.280
Ted Cruz will have a bounce back. He has now&nbsp;
four years to start getting on all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:52.800
tired trope. So we've got to fight socialism.&nbsp;
And the debt is, you know, all that stuff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:59.600
He'll have a bounce back. There will be some,&nbsp;
you know, tragedy of some kind that will allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:05.760
him to talk about how the left wants to take your&nbsp;
guns. And if he wants to be reelected, he almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:12.240
certainly will. If we leave it to Republicans in&nbsp;
Texas to decide and remember the things he did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:19.520
the way to defeat Ted Cruz is to get out&nbsp;
the vote and to register new voters. And if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:25.280
this reelection campaign for Cruz were maybe in&nbsp;
twenty twenty two, there'd be more of a shot at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:29.520
the Cancun fiasco, having an impact. If it were&nbsp;
twenty, twenty one, it would be even better.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:37.840
But the cautious optimism for twenty twenty four&nbsp;
is that Cruz did only defeat Beto O'Rourke by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:43.280
a small margin, about fifty one to forty eight in&nbsp;
twenty eighteen. If the election were now or in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:48.000
few months and we were hammering, hammering,&nbsp;
hammering on get out the vote and registering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:53.280
voters, I think that overall would have a real&nbsp;
shot at defeating Ted Cruz four years from now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.440
makes it more difficult to predict. But let's&nbsp;
prove ourselves wrong. If Ted Cruz is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:03.920
to be defeated on twenty twenty four, it will&nbsp;
be because of the campaign that we mount. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:09.680
we saw the model in Georgia with Stacey Abrams&nbsp;
and registering new new voters, Joe Biden winning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:15.760
Georgia, two Democratic challengers in the Senate&nbsp;
in Georgia, defeating two Republican incumbents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:22.560
in Georgia so it can be done. We've seen the&nbsp;
playbook simply waiting for the Cancun fiasco to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:27.360
effect Republicans four years from now. That's&nbsp;
not the winning strategy. I'm open to having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:31.120
my mind changed, but I've seen no evidence&nbsp;
that that's the way to win that seat back.

